            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bob and Mary are discussing the days that they were born. They both know that at least one of them was born on a weekend. Find the probability that they were both born on the weekend.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac{1}{6}$",
      "worked": "Use independent births with each day of the week equally likely. There are $7\\times7=49$ ordered day pairs. Of these, $5\\times5=25$ have neither birth on a weekend, while $2\\times2=4$ have both births on a weekend.\n$$\\begin{align*}n(\\text{at least one weekend birth})&=49-25=24\\\\P(\\text{both weekend}\\mid\\text{at least one weekend})&=\\frac{4}{24}\\\\P(\\text{both weekend}\\mid\\text{at least one weekend})&=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 54
  },
